  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to find a step function minimizing the maximum overlap (auto and cross) with its shift. The given problem is a non-convex optimization over a constrained space. We leverage multiple strategies to escape local minima: (a) restarted optimization from multiple starting points, (b) binary representation (for feasible computation) with iterative refinement, (c) continuous optimization to explore the full range, and (d) directional gradient-based updates. The original solution used grid descent and grid expansion but failed to break the record. We improve by combining: i) more aggressive gradient projection with corrected subgradient, ii) binary search for integer solutions (even though fractional might be better, binary often finds global minima), iii) execution priority optimization, iv) resolution adaptation.\n\nKey limitations: \n- Subgradient descent seems susceptible to saddle points.\n- Binary search might not find the minimal fraction approximation.\n- Higher resolution requires more evaluations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nObjective: minimize C5 = max_k (dx * correlation_full(h, 1-h)), with dx=2.0/n, n=len(h)\nConstraints: 0 <= h[i] <= 1, sum(h) = n/2\n\nRepresentation: vector h \u2208 \u211d^n with dx=2.0/n.\n\nConstraints: all elements in [0,1] and sum to n/2.\n\nDerivatives:\nFor a given lag k (from -n+1 to n-1), the integral is approximated as an affine function in h. The subgradient of each lag term is:\n\n    \u2207(lag term) = (2/n) * [ (1-h[i+k]) - h[i] * (if considering shift effects) ]\n\nThe full gradient is the component-wise maximum.\n\nValid candidates must satisfy:\n    for all i: 0 <= h[i] <= 1\n    sum(i) h[i] = n/2\n\nUsed solvers: SLSQP (with analytic constraint), projected gradient (with steps rejected if not feasible)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Initialization: start from initial_h_values (n=250) and other binary-initialized vectors\n- Optimization phases:\n  1. Randomized grid expansion (n=100, 200, 250, 500)\n  2. Initial local search with gradient projection on batches of lags (1000-10000 iterations)\n  3. Binary refinement: start from fraction-adjusted binary vectors\n  4. Progressive reduction to avoid boundary artifacts\n\nKey operations:\n- Projection: clip to [0,1] and adjust sum to n/2\n- Lag sample set: priorities by common and spectral heuristics\n- Batch gradient estimation by subset (up to 100 lags at a time)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nStart configurations: \n- n_list = [100, 200, 250, 500]\n- Initializations: random uniform [0,1] with sum(n/2), random binary with exactly n/2 ones, and more aggressive perturbations.\n\nParameter scheduling:\n- Each grid is solved for max 30% of budget (1100s corresponds to ~330 seconds each)\n- Learning rate decays as inverse square of lag dataset\n- Projective gradient step size adaptive:\n  - Start=0.1, decay schedule based on gradient norm\n- Lag sampling: initially uniform, then increasingly concentrated on high-k and low-k pairs\n\nFallback: use initial_h_values if domain exhausted or no improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEstimated time per grid n: O(n*T) where T is steps, typically 300-1000 steps per grid.\nThe worst-case is n=500, which would require ~1500 steps \u00d7 500 operations = 750,000 operations \u2013 feasible in under 10 seconds.\n\nTermination checks:\n- No improvement across 50 steps in candidate\n- Time limit (1100 seconds)\n- Consecutive non-improving gradients (10 steps)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal candidate:\n- Compute C5 directly by correlation\n- Check sum(h*i) == n/2 (within tolerance)\n- Ensure all values in [0,1] (within bounds tolerance)\n\nThese conditions must hold.\n\nEvaluate using the provided correlation method scaled by dx.",
